[Session 5 — 2026-06-07]
  Plugin module + Command palette + Animation performance + Build warning cleanup
  (Implements requirement #2 "Plugin module" from Change request.md, plus a new command palette)

  1) Python script plugin module (hot-pluggable, no C# recompile):
     New plugins/ directory; drop in a .py file to add a command. The kernel
     scans and loads them automatically on startup.
     Plugin contract: COMMAND (command name) / DESCRIPTION (text) / run(args) -> str
     New files:
       plugins/__init__.py          — package marker (empty)
       plugins/example_hello.py     — sample plugin (the "hello" command)
     kernel/openncl_kernel.py:
       + __init__ calls _load_plugins(): importlib scans plugins/*.py,
         validates COMMAND + run, stores into self.plugins; failures are
         recorded in plugin_errors without crashing.
       + exec() dispatch chain matches plugins by first token at the end
         (after URL detection) and runs them.
       + New "plugins" command: lists loaded plugins + load errors.
     MainWindow.xaml.cs: Exec() routes "plugins" -> __FORWARD__ (to kernel).
       (Other plugin commands flow through the "unknown command fallback
        forward" path, so they work automatically — no per-command branch.)
     Works on both native and Python paths: both import the same kernel,
     so logic only needs to live in the kernel.

  2) WinUI command palette (Ctrl+Shift+P, VSCode-style):
     MainWindow.xaml: added CommandPaletteOverlay at the end of RootGrid
       (translucent scrim + centered panel: search TextBox + command ListView).
     MainWindow.xaml.cs:
       + CommandCatalog: ~50 commands with descriptions as the search source.
       + RootGrid_KeyDown intercepts Ctrl+Shift+P -> OpenCommandPalette().
       + Search box: TextChanged fuzzy filter / Up-Down move selection /
         Enter confirm / Esc close.
       + Selected command -> inserted into the terminal input line (not
         auto-executed, so args can be appended) -> focus returns to terminal.
       + Click scrim to close; click panel does not close (Tapped bubbling).
     Modifier key detection: InputKeyboardSource.GetKeyStateForCurrentThread.

  3) Performance — reuse animation brushes:
     MainWindow.xaml.cs: the 4 background orbs previously allocated a new
       RadialGradientBrush + 2 GradientStops every frame (~720 objects/sec,
       constant GC pressure).
     Now: InitRainbow creates the 4 brushes once, caches them in _orbBrushes[]
       and binds orb.Fill. ApplyOrb only updates position/size and mutates
       GradientStops[].Color on the cached brush — no more allocations.
       -> Zero allocation on the animation hot path.

  4) Build warning cleanup:
     - NETSDK1198 (missing win-AnyCPU.pubxml, prior known issue #5):
       Added Condition="'$(Platform)'!='AnyCPU'" to the csproj PublishProfile.
       No profile is specified under AnyCPU, clearing the warning; x64/arm64/x86
       publishing still works correctly.
     - PRI249 (CPYTHON-311 invalid resource qualifier):
       Caused by __pycache__/*.pyc being captured by the Content wildcard.
       Added Exclude="...\__pycache__\**" to each Python Content dir, so .pyc
       caches are no longer packaged.

  5) Fixed paste bug (Ctrl+V did not work):
     Root cause: the terminal uses self-drawn input (RootGrid KeyDown /
       CharReceived + _inputBuf), with no real TextBox. KeyDown did not handle
       Ctrl+V, and CharReceived returned early on control chars < 0x20
       (Ctrl+V produces 0x16), so clipboard content never reached the buffer.
     MainWindow.xaml.cs:
       + RootGrid_KeyDown intercepts Ctrl+V -> PasteFromClipboard().
       + PasteFromClipboard(): Clipboard.GetContent().GetTextAsync() reads text;
         since terminal input is single-line, newlines/tabs are collapsed to
         spaces, appended to _inputBuf, and the prompt line is refreshed.
     (The palette search box PaletteSearch is a native TextBox and AiSidebar
      is a WebView2 — both have built-in paste and are unaffected; this change
      only covers the main terminal input area.)

